WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.102
-Is it alright,
sitting back like this?

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:04.803
Too casual, too...?

00:00:04.803 --> 00:00:07.139
The first time I auditioned,
I remember walking

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:08.808
underneath all these pipes
that you've seen,

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:11.177
you know, in sketches, whenever
they do backstage things.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.879
I go, "This is really surreal."

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.480
Like I was here once before.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.348
My Uncle George,
when he played Norm on "Cheers"

00:00:16.348 --> 00:00:18.517 line:0%
and he hosted the finale
of 1991,

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:19.919 line:0%
so I got to come watch
that show

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.054 line:0%
and so this was the second time
I got to be,

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:24.157
you know, in the space
and here it was,

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:25.759
you know, 12 years later,
a dozen years later.

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:27.760
And then turn and then
there's the stage

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.562
and the brass are sitting there
at a table

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.330
and you're just doing it,
but to no laughs.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.632
You're like, "Ah, [bleep]
Alright, okay."

00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:35.535
[ Laughs ]

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:40.507
♪♪

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:42.542 line:0%
I guess it was like August 2003,

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:46.278 line:0%
I was doing a Second City show
in Las Vegas at the Flamingo.

00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:47.814 line:0%
We were performing
for no one.

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.348 line:0%
We were doing shows
for like eight people.

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:50.650 line:0%
I remember Jeff Richmond,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.451 line:0%
Tina Fey's husband,
who did music here

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:54.053 line:0%
for many moons, I remember him
saying to me, he was like,

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:55.955
"Would you ever want to audition
for 'Saturday Night Live'"?

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:59.725
And I was like,
"I don't know. Why? No. Why?"

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:01.227
You know,
and the way I've always said it,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.662
it's like the prettiest girl
at school,

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.096
you know, you're like,
"Oh, I hate her,"

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:05.264
and then like,
"Oh, that's too bad.

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:06.498
She has a crush on you."
"Really?!"

00:01:06.498 --> 00:01:07.933
The first time I auditioned was

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.302
at Stand Up NY.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.004
There was 12 guys.
A guy named Rob Riggle.

00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:12.204
-Rob Riggle.
-J.B. Smoove.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:13.539
-Aah!
-Finesse Mitchell.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:14.808
-[ Crying ] "SNL" sold me out."

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.376
-Kenan, who got hired
at that time.

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.110
-Yeah, my name is
Kenan Thompson. How y'all doing?

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.246
-But then, I get up
to go do my thing

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:22.548
and, right before I go up,
Chris Rock;

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:24.417
and Jeff Ross, you know,
the RoastMaster General,

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.653
come walking in and then,
the guy hosting the thing

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:28.554
was like, "We got a special
surprise for you.

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:30.456
Ladies and gentlemen, put your
hands together for Chris Rock!"

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:33.426
And Chris looks at me
[ Laughing ] and he goes,

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:34.727
"Was this your big shot, man?"

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.996
I go, "Yeah, kinda."
He goes, "Sorry,"

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.631
and then he goes out there,
you know, the place goes nuts.

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:41.334
Then the guy onstage is like,
"Ladies and gentlemen,

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.502
put your hands together.
Next comic, Jason "Sudehkis."

00:01:43.502 --> 00:01:44.837
You know, I go up there.
I said,

00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:46.072
"It's actually pronounced
'Sudaykis,'

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.407
not that anybody cares, but,

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:48.975
alright, here we go."
It went well.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.277
It felt like it went well.
Got done with that and then,

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.745
we got a call the next day,
saying, "Hey,

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:53.980
they're going
to ask you to test,"

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:54.881 line:0%
and then did the same thing

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.115 line:0%
at home base here.

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:57.516 line:0%
I mean, it was,
you know, surreal.

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.452 line:0%
Alright. Jason Sudei--

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.721 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, Jason Sudeikis.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.522 line:0%
And then got on a flight,
went home, found out

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:05.458
a week later, "They want
to fly you back to meet Lorne."

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.627
I sit down.
He sits next to me and goes --

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.229
[ As Michaels ]
So, what era of the show
did you grow up with?

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.497
And I just blanked.
And I said,

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.933
"I really loved Eddie Murphy."

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.136
Now, sure, I picked the one
famous dude that guy didn't hire

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:19.706
and he gets the sense
that I'm not a fan of the show,

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.574
and he has this great line
where he just goes --

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:23.175
[ As Michaels ]
Oh, don't worry.
I didn't watch the show

00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:24.276
when I didn't work on it,
either.

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:25.578
[ Laughs ]

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:27.446
One of the first times that
that dude

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:29.181
bailed me out of my own [bleep]

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:31.384
Later that night, we got a call
from Steve Higgins, saying,

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.585
"Hey, we're going to hire you
as a writer."

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:34.353
I'm like, "[Bleep] I blew it."

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.489
[ Laughs ]

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:39.125
I was confused because I had
just figured out how

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:40.159
to write for myself.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.594
I had imposter syndrome.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.629
And Tina, she was a hero of mine
at Second City.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:45.464
She was like, you know, "If you
can improvise, you can write."

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:46.899
And then her other piece
of advice was like,

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:48.968
"Don't write anything
for someone in the cast

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.336
that you could do yourself."

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.737
At this point,
she was on "Update,"

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:53.572
so she had walked
that path herself.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:55.842
It was a learning process
of how to develop my voice,

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.844
but have it come
out through other people

00:02:57.844 --> 00:02:59.311
and I got lucky.

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:01.380
I had two sketches
on my first two weeks,

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.782 line:0%
one with Jack Black.

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:04.484 line:0%
And then, the second week
was Justin Timberlake,

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:05.952
the first time he hosted,
and I wrote this sketch,

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.787 line:0%
it was a parody of "Punk'd."
-I know I am.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.822 line:0%
I'm Ashton Kutcher.
I'm awesome!

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.925 line:0%
-And Timberlake just crushed.
And so I was like two for two.

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:15.761
Then, Week 3, I think I got
to write with Robert Smigel

00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:17.830 line:0%
and write a thing
for my Uncle George

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.164 line:0%
to play the Superfans.

00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:23.002 line:0%
-♪ Come on, babe,
why don't we paint the town? ♪

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:23.936
-But it was like kind of like

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:25.137
I landed and dropped in

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:26.172
and just off to the races.

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:27.440 line:0%
Hi, I'm a I'm a big fan

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.941 line:0%
of your new sitcom,
"Hope &amp; Faith."

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:30.375 line:0%
-Oh, wow! Thank you!

00:03:30.375 --> 00:03:32.545 line:0%
-Yeah, no.
I was just kidding.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.080
And then, I went like
10 weeks with jack [bleep]

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.148
I loved the rewrite table.

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:37.383 line:0%
The rewrite table was the best.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:38.751 line:0%
It was like joking around
with friends

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:40.486 line:0%
and you get to, you know,
mumble like a joke pitch

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:41.653 line:0%
under my mouth and Tina would be
like, [ Snaps fingers ]

00:03:41.653 --> 00:03:43.088
"What was that?
That's great.

00:03:43.088 --> 00:03:45.291
Okay, we're going with that."
You know, like,

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.926
"Alright.
Oh, I'll be darned."

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.894
And again, the imposter syndrome
was so prevalent,

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:51.397
it only helped fill the thing up
to be like,

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.365
"You belong, you belong,
you belong here."

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:54.600
And yet, at the same time,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.269
when I'd have those lows,
I'd be like,

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.537
"Why did I stop performing?

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:59.005
Why did I stop performing?"

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.574
So, after the first year
and everything went decent,

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.375
then, the summer was off
and then,

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:05.544
come back
and I auditioned again.

00:04:05.544 --> 00:04:07.279 line:0%
George W. Bush doing
an abbreviated

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:08.814 line:0%
stump speech in Wisconsin.

00:04:08.814 --> 00:04:10.282 line:0%
[ As Bush ] It's nice to be
around some Cheeseheads

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:11.317 line:0%
that aren't in Washington.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:12.652 line:0%
[ Laughs ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.553 line:0%
-Don't get it.
Then, I was asked to test

00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:16.388 line:0%
for "Weekend Update."

00:04:16.388 --> 00:04:17.990 line:0%
I'm Jason Sudeikis.
-I'm Tina Fey

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.293 line:0%
and here are
tonight's top stories.

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:22.227
-And that was fun.
Amy got it, Amy Poehler.

00:04:22.227 --> 00:04:23.896
Again, no complaints.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.598
And so, then, it was just right
back into the writing thing.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:26.699
And I'd get asked to play,
you know,

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.167
like someone in the monologue

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:29.668
or like a cop in the background,

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:31.737
but, at some point,
I remember there were like

00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:34.707
five shows left
my second season.

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:36.875
I asked Lorne if I could talk
to him on Friday night.

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:38.377
You always want to hit him
after dinner.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:39.512
People have drinks at dinner.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.280
I say, "Lorne, this has been

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.949
an amazing experience,

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.818
but I had to give up two things
that I love the most,

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.287
and that was living with my wife
and performing."

00:04:48.287 --> 00:04:50.723
And he just looks at me.
He's pouring water.
He's just like --

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.325
[ As Michaels ]
Well, if you had to choose one?

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:54.627
[ Laughs ]
You know, and I was like --

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:55.961
And I just started laughing.
And he goes --

00:04:55.961 --> 00:04:57.396
[ As Michaels ]
No, I understand.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:59.098
And we just sort of
left it there.

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:01.668
It was by no means me throwing
down the gantlet, like,

00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:03.702
"Hey, you put me in the cast
or I --

00:05:03.702 --> 00:05:05.304
I take my bad sketches

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:06.638
[ Laughing ]
and I go elsewhere."

00:05:06.638 --> 00:05:09.008
Now, that week also happened
to coincide

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:10.610
with Tom Brady hosting.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.578
John Lutz, Rich Talarico,
and Joe Kelly,

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:13.980
we all wrote this
"Behind the Music"

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.482
for the "Super Bowl Shuffle"
for the '85 Bears

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.350
and, in that sketch,
we were playing

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.186
all the white dudes
on the Bears.

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:21.454
Tom Brady played Jim McMahon.

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:22.688 line:0%
So the premise was
that Jim McMahon,

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:23.956 line:0%
they did
the "Super Bowl Shuffle."

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:25.425 line:0%
It was a huge success.
Then, he tried

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:26.993
to do another one.
Then he went solo.

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:30.596
And, when he went solo,
I would just dance behind him.

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:33.933
And that same week,
Beck was the musical guest.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:36.869
He, for some reason, had a guy,
like a hype man, next to him

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:39.272
and was just dancing buck wild,
just going crazy,

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:41.641
so then I was like, "Oh, I'll
just do my version of that,"

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:44.343
which is like doing
all this '90s hip hop dance

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:46.646
that I knew how to do from being
like one of the few white kids

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:48.614
on a predominantly Black
basketball team

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:50.449
in my high school
basketball days.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:52.718
That sketch was in rehearsal.

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:55.754
So you had 20 dudes
that were onstage,

00:05:55.754 --> 00:05:58.791
who are now off stage,
who are now watching me do this,

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.693
and I remember them
all laughing.

00:06:00.693 --> 00:06:02.495
I remember Lorne walking in.

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:03.830
It kind of became
like a spotlight thing,

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.731
something a cast member
should be doing,

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:07.734
which I totally agree with.

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:09.101
And so, then,
Friday night meeting,

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:10.869
and they're talking
about recasting that part

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:12.571
and Tina's like,
"Well, who's going

00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:14.106
to get those laughs?"
So Lorne's like --

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:15.407
[ As Michaels ]
Okay, we'll just have him do it.

00:06:15.407 --> 00:06:16.442
So then, cut to Saturday.

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:17.910
My parents happen to be there.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.145 line:0%
-♪ Struttin' for fun ♪

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:20.079 line:0%
♪ Struttin' my stuff ♪

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:21.781 line:0%
♪ For everyone ♪

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:23.749
-Cut to, you know,
two weeks off.

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:25.317
I get a call.
It's Lorne's office.

00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:26.752
"Lorne wants to talk to you."

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.053
I was like, "Okay."

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:29.789
I wait like 30 minutes.
I'm kind of like,

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:31.023
"What's going on?"
What's going on?"

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:33.459
And I call and he's like,
you know --

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:36.595
[ As Michaels ]
So, we want to put you
in the cast

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:38.631
and, um, you know,

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:40.799
just write anything
you think you can score on."

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:42.701
You know, I was like, "Okay."

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:44.337
[ As Michaels ]
Okay. That's it. Bye.

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:46.872
There's three episodes left
and it's just like, "Alright."

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:49.875
I was like, "Thank you
for changing my life."

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:52.278 line:0%
♪♪

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:54.447 line:0%
So, yeah, that's how I ended up
in the cast.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:55.914 line:0%
It's that same
friggin' dancing

00:06:55.914 --> 00:06:57.516 line:0%
that people love
from "What Up with That?"

00:06:57.516 --> 00:07:00.419 line:0%
It's the same friggin' dance
that I did as a 16-year-old kid,

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:01.620
trying to make
my teammates laugh.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.423
It's like, you know.

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:08.661
So, leaving here, I was maybe
going to leave in 2012,

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:10.229
when Kristen and Andy left.

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:12.231
I wasn't sure
and I wasn't sure that night

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:15.067
and that was really, really hard
because, one, I'm saying goodbye

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:17.069
to friends, people that I had,
you know,

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:18.604
grown up with, you know,
at that point,

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.139
a, you know, quarter of my life.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.508
We're all very close
and connected,

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.776
and especially Kristen and I.

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:25.978 line:0%
Having to say goodbye
to that friend

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.981 line:0%
on live television,
it was a lot.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.017 line:0%
And, again, not knowing
if I was going to go,

00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:33.786
but it just -- ah!

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:38.190
So then, ended up deciding
to come back for 2012-2013.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:39.792
Our group was leaving.
You know, I knew Seth was going

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:41.226
to be leaving.
Fred was leaving.

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:42.761
I knew Bill was leaving
and I knew I was leaving.

00:07:42.761 --> 00:07:44.263
I just didn't really
tell anybody.

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:46.065
It was like, "Who's going to be
across the table?"

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:47.899
And I didn't want to be
a bad upperclassman,

00:07:47.899 --> 00:07:50.937
a [bleep] senior, being like,
"It was better this."

00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:53.872
Cynicism, it's a currency
we spend here,

00:07:53.872 --> 00:07:56.075
but is not one
that has a great exchange rate.

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:58.009
And I wrote a sketch,
kind of a goodbye sketch.

00:07:58.009 --> 00:08:00.046
It was like where I play
the strip club DJ and it was --

00:08:00.046 --> 00:08:02.648
[ Raspy voice ]
His last night there.
Now, we gonna head out.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.450 line:0%
Going to miss the hell
out of all you dummies

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.717 line:0%
and, if I could do it all
over again,

00:08:05.717 --> 00:08:07.286 line:0%
I wouldn't change a thing.

00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:08.487
So long!

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:09.821
And it didn't get picked.

00:08:09.821 --> 00:08:11.356 line:0%
It didn't get picked
for the final show.

00:08:11.356 --> 00:08:12.624 line:0%
So, it was just like,

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:14.093
"Oh, son of a bitch."

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:16.195
I wish I would've just been
a writer the whole time,

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:17.329 line:0%
you know.
[ Laughs ]

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:19.498 line:0%
And so, yeah, I left.

00:08:19.498 --> 00:08:21.133 line:0%
♪♪

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:23.869 line:0%
To get to work here 10 years,
in the rearview mirror,

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:27.439
I feel like I nailed the entry
and the exit for me

00:08:27.439 --> 00:08:30.309
because I got to learn the place
as a writer, first and foremost.

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:33.846
I was just, you know, trying
to keep my head above water

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:36.382
and keep up with
what I would argue is one

00:08:36.382 --> 00:08:37.750
of the greatest generations

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.085
of cast and writers
that the show has had

00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:46.792
and I was just so lucky to get
to be at that table, yeah.

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.862
This place changed my life
twice, twice.

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:52.498 line:0%
Once, as a cast member,
as a writer here.

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:57.136 line:0%
But, most importantly, as a kid,
watching from home, you know.

00:08:57.136 --> 00:08:59.204 line:0%
And I'll tell you this --
if this is your first time

00:08:59.204 --> 00:09:03.142 line:0%
watching this show,
well, I mean,

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:05.945
well, this one probably won't
change your life, you know?

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:07.680
But we got a great show
for you tonight.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.915
Brandi Carlile is here!

00:09:09.915 --> 00:09:11.984
So stick around.
We'll be right back.

00:09:11.984 --> 00:09:15.955
♪♪

